(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the chemical equilibria of Ocean Acidification, the decline in carbonate saturation state \(Omega\), and the physiological mechanisms of coral bleaching.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Ocean Acidification Geochemistry, Carbonate Speciation, and Coral Bleaching Dynamics:) Tj
T*
(   1. Aquatic Carbonate Chemical Equilibria:) Tj
T*
(   Anthropogenic CO2 emitted into the atmosphere dissolves into surface seawater according to) Tj
T*
(   Henry's Law:) Tj
T*
(   CO2 \(g\) <-> CO2 \(aq\)) Tj
T*
(   Dissolved CO2 reacts with water to form carbonic acid:) Tj
T*
(   CO2 \(aq\) + H2O <-> H2CO3) Tj
T*
(   Carbonic acid rapidly dissociates into hydrogen ions and bicarbonate:) Tj
T*
(   H2CO3 <-> H+ + HCO3-) Tj
T*
(   The accumulating hydrogen ions \(H+\) lower seawater pH \(ocean acidification: surface pH has) Tj
T*
(   declined by 0.11 units from pre-industrial 8.25 to 8.14, representing a 29% increase in H+) Tj
T*
(   acidity\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Depletion of Carbonate Ions \([CO3^2-]\):) Tj
T*
(   The surplus hydrogen ions react with ambient carbonate ions, converting them into bicarbonate:) Tj
T*
(   H+ + CO3^2- <-> HCO3-) Tj
T*
(   Consequently, the concentration of free carbonate ions \([CO3^2-]\) drops precipitously!) Tj
T*
(   3. Calcium Carbonate Saturation State \(Omega\):) Tj
T*
(   Omega = [Ca2+] * [CO3^2-] / K'_sp) Tj
T*
(   Where K'_sp is the apparent stoichiometric solubility product for calcium carbonate minerals) Tj
T*
(   \(aragonite or calcite\).) Tj
T*
(   - When Omega > 1.0: Seawater is supersaturated; calcification is thermodynamically favored.) Tj
T*
(   - When Omega < 1.0: Seawater is under-saturated; existing calcium carbonate shells) Tj
T*
(   spontaneously dissolve!) Tj
T*
(   - Marine calcifiers \(pteropods, foraminifera, reef-building scleractinian corals\) secrete) Tj
T*
(   delicate aragonite skeletons. As Omega_aragonite declines toward 1.0, the energetic cost of) Tj
T*
