/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the atmospheric dynamics and heterogeneous chemistry of the Antarctic Ozone Hole, detailing Polar Stratospheric Clouds \(PSCs\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Atmospheric Dynamics and Heterogeneous Chemistry of the Antarctic Ozone Hole:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Polar Stratospheric Chlorine Paradox:) Tj
T*
(   In the global mid-latitude stratosphere, reactive chlorine radicals \(Cl. and ClO.\) are) Tj
T*
(   continuously neutralized into unreactive, non-ozone-depleting 'reservoir species':) Tj
T*
(   Cl. + CH4 -> HCl + .CH3) Tj
T*
(   ClO. + NO2 + M -> ClONO2 + M \(Chlorine nitrate\).) Tj
T*
(   Under these conditions, active chlorine accounts for < 1% of total stratospheric chlorine. Why) Tj
T*
(   then does catastrophic 70-90% ozone depletion occur over Antarctica every austral spring) Tj
T*
(   \(September-October\)?) Tj
T*
(   2. Meteorological Pre-Condition: The Polar Vortex and Extreme Cold:) Tj
T*
(   - During the perpetual darkness of the Antarctic polar winter \(June-August\), strong circumpolar) Tj
T*
(   stratospheric winds isolate the Antarctic air mass into a gigantic spinning cyclone: the Polar) Tj
T*
(   Vortex.) Tj
T*
(   - The vortex prevents mid-latitude warm air and ozone-rich air from entering. Radiative cooling) Tj
T*
(   drops stratospheric temperatures below -80 to -88 deg C \(185 to 193 K\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Polar Stratospheric Clouds \(PSCs\):) Tj
T*
(   At these extreme sub-zero temperatures, trace stratospheric vapors condense into Polar) Tj
T*
(   Stratospheric Clouds:) Tj
T*
(   - Type I PSCs \(T < 195 K\): Composed of crystalline Nitric Acid Trihydrate \(NAT, HNO3.3H2O\) and) Tj
T*
(   supercooled ternary solutions \(H2SO4 / HNO3 / H2O\).) Tj
T*
(   - Type II PSCs \(T < 188 K\): Composed of pure water ice crystals.) Tj
T*
(   4. Heterogeneous Chlorine Activation Reactions:) Tj
T*
(   The ice crystal surfaces of PSCs catalyze rapid, heterogeneous chemical reactions between) Tj
T*
(   reservoir species:) Tj
T*
(   - Reaction 1: ClONO2 \(g\) + HCl \(ice\) --[PSC]--> Cl2 \(g\) + HNO3 \(ice\)) Tj
T*
(   - Reaction 2: ClONO2 \(g\) + H2O \(ice\) --[PSC]--> HOCl \(g\) + HNO3 \(ice\)) Tj
T*
(   - Reaction 3: HOCl \(g\) + HCl \(ice\) --[PSC]--> Cl2 \(g\) + H2O \(ice\).) Tj
T*
(   Significance: Unreactive reservoir species are converted into molecular chlorine gas \(Cl2\) and) Tj
T*
(   hypochlorous acid \(HOCl\), which evaporate into the gas phase.) Tj
T*
(   5. Denoxification and Austral Spring Ozone Destruction:) Tj
T*
(   - Nitric acid \(HNO3\) remains frozen inside large PSC ice crystals. Heavy crystals settle out) Tj
T*
(   under gravity into the lower stratosphere \(Denitrification\).) Tj
T*
(   - In September, the return of solar sunlight photolyzes Cl2 into a flood of active chlorine) Tj
T*
(   radicals:) Tj
T*
(   Cl2 + h*nu -> 2 Cl.) Tj
T*
(   - Because denitrification has removed gaseous NO2, reactive chlorine cannot be sequestered back) Tj
T*
(   into ClONO2!) Tj
T*
(   - Active chlorine destroys ozone via the ClO dimer cycle \(Molina-Molina mechanism\):) Tj
T*
(   2 [ Cl. + O3 -> ClO. + O2 ]) Tj
T*
(   ClO + ClO + M -> Cl2O2 + M \(Dichlorine dioxide\)) Tj
T*
(   Cl2O2 + h*nu -> ClOO + Cl.) Tj
T*
(   ClOO + M -> Cl. + O2 + M) Tj
T*
(   Net: 2 O3 -> 3 O2.) Tj
T*
(   - Ozone inside the polar vortex collapses within 3-4 weeks until the vortex breaks up in late) Tj
T*
(   November.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

