(   Soviet bloc\). Subject to legally binding emission caps \(Assigned Amount Units, AAUs\).) Tj
T*
(   - Non-Annex I Parties: Developing nations \(including India, China, Brazil\). Exempted from) Tj
T*
(   binding emission caps during the first commitment period to prioritize economic poverty) Tj
T*
(   eradication.) Tj
T*
(   3. The Three Flexible Market-Based Mechanisms:) Tj
T*
(   Designed to lower the economic cost of achieving emission targets by allowing Annex I countries) Tj
T*
(   to achieve emission reductions extraterritorially where marginal abatement costs are lowest:) Tj
T*
(   - 1. Clean Development Mechanism \(CDM, Article 12\):) Tj
T*
(   * Operational Mechanism: An Annex I government or corporation invests in an emission-reduction) Tj
T*
(   or renewable energy project located in a Non-Annex I developing country \(e.g., building a wind) Tj
T*
(   farm, solar park, or biomass plant in India\).) Tj
T*
(   * Certification: The project must satisfy the 'Additionality' criterion \(proving emissions are) Tj
T*
(   lower than baseline business-as-usual\).) Tj
T*
(   * Output: The UN CDM Executive Board issues Certified Emission Reductions \(CERs, where 1 CER =) Tj
T*
(   1 metric ton CO2e reduced\). Annex I parties use CERs to offset domestic compliance targets.) Tj
T*
(   - 2. Joint Implementation \(JI, Article 6\):) Tj
T*
(   * An Annex I country finances an emission-reduction project in another Annex I country) Tj
T*
(   \(primarily Eastern European EIT states\).) Tj
T*
(   * Output: Generates Emission Reduction Units \(ERUs\).) Tj
T*
(   - 3. International Emissions Trading \(IET, Article 17\):) Tj
T*
(   * 'Cap-and-Trade': An Annex I party that reduces its emissions below its statutory cap can sell) Tj
T*
(   its surplus Assigned Amount Units \(AAUs\) to another Annex I country that is struggling to meet) Tj
T*
(   its compliance targets.) Tj
