(   [Problem Statement]: Compare the Representative Concentration Pathways \(RCPs\) and Shared Socioeconomic Pathways \(SSPs\) used in IPCC Assessment Reports.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   IPCC Climate Scenario Modeling: RCPs, SSPs, and Coupled Earth System Trajectories:) Tj
T*
(   1. Representative Concentration Pathways \(RCPs, IPCC AR5\):) Tj
T*
(   RCPs model future greenhouse gas atmospheric concentration trajectories and their resulting) Tj
T*
(   radiative forcing by the year 2100 relative to pre-industrial baselines:) Tj
T*
(   - RCP 2.6 \(Stringent Decarbonization\): Peak-and-decline scenario where radiative forcing peaks) Tj
T*
(   at ~3.0 W/m2 mid-century and declines to 2.6 W/m2 by 2100. Requires massive immediate carbon) Tj
T*
(   reductions; limits global warming to ~1.5 to 2.0 deg C.) Tj
T*
(   - RCP 4.5 \(Intermediate Stabilization\): Radiative forcing stabilizes at 4.5 W/m2 by 2100) Tj
T*
(   without overshoot. Global temperature rise projected at ~2.0 to 3.0 deg C.) Tj
T*
(   - RCP 6.0 \(Intermediate / Delayed Action\): Radiative forcing stabilizes at 6.0 W/m2 after 2100) Tj
T*
(   through heavy reliance on fossil fuels followed by late mitigation.) Tj
T*
(   - RCP 8.5 \(High Emissions / Baseline\): High fossil fuel energy intensive pathway leading to) Tj
T*
(   continuous radiative forcing reaching 8.5 W/m2 by 2100 \(CO2 concentrations exceeding 1,000) Tj
T*
(   ppm\). Global warming projected at 4.0 to 5.5 deg C, causing catastrophic sea-level rise and) Tj
T*
(   climatic destabilization.) Tj
T*
(   2. Shared Socioeconomic Pathways \(SSPs, IPCC AR6\):) Tj
T*
(   SSPs model five plausible narratives of future global societal, economic, and demographic) Tj
T*
(   developments, evaluating challenges to climate mitigation versus adaptation:) Tj
T*
(   - SSP1 \(Sustainability - 'Taking the Green Road'\): Rapid technological development, low) Tj
T*
(   population growth, high investments in education and clean energy. Low mitigation and low) Tj
T*
(   adaptation challenges.) Tj
T*
(   - SSP2 \(Middle of the Road\): Historical trends continue; moderate development and uneven) Tj
T*
(   progress.) Tj
T*
(   - SSP3 \(Regional Rivalry - 'A Rocky Road'\): Resurgent nationalism, fragmented regional trade) Tj
T*
(   blocs, high population growth in developing regions, heavy reliance on domestic coal. High) Tj
T*
(   challenges to both mitigation and adaptation.) Tj
T*
(   - SSP4 \(Inequality - 'A Road Divided'\): Deepening inequalities between an internationally) Tj
T*
(   connected high-tech elite and fragmented impoverished populations. Low challenge to mitigation,) Tj
T*
(   high challenge to adaptation.) Tj
T*
(   - SSP5 \(Fossil-Fueled Development - 'Taking the Highway'\): Faith in competitive free markets) Tj
T*
(   and technological innovation driven by massive exploitation of abundant fossil fuel reserves.) Tj
T*
(   High challenge to mitigation, low challenge to adaptation.) Tj
T*
(   3. The Coupled Scenario Matrix:) Tj
T*
(   Modern IPCC simulations combine narratives \(e.g., SSP1-2.6 models a sustainable green society) Tj
T*
(   achieving 2.6 W/m2; SSP5-8.5 models an unconstrained fossil-fuel boom achieving 8.5 W/m2\).) Tj
