(   Constitutional Environmental Jurisprudence of India: Fundamental Rights, Duties, and Judicial) Tj
T*
(   Precedents:) Tj
T*
(   1. Constitutional Foundations \(The 42nd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1976\):) Tj
T*
(   The 42nd Amendment introduced explicit environmental conservation obligations into the) Tj
T*
(   Constitution of India:) Tj
T*
(   - Article 48A \(Directive Principles of State Policy\):) Tj
T*
(   'The State shall endeavour to protect and improve the environment and to safeguard the forests) Tj
T*
(   and wild life of the country.' Imposes an affirmative constitutional mandate upon legislative) Tj
T*
(   and executive branches.) Tj
T*
(   - Article 51A\(g\) \(Fundamental Duties\):) Tj
T*
(   'It shall be the duty of every citizen of India to protect and improve the natural environment) Tj
T*
(   including forests, lakes, rivers and wild life, and to have compassion for living creatures.') Tj
T*
(   2. Judicial Expansion of Article 21 \(The Right to Life\):) Tj
T*
(   Article 21 guarantees that 'No person shall be deprived of his life or personal liberty except) Tj
T*
(   according to procedure established by law.') Tj
T*
(   Through creative judicial activism, the Supreme Court of India broadened the meaning of 'Life') Tj
T*
(   beyond mere animal existence to encompass the Right to a Wholesome, Clean, and Pollution-Free) Tj
T*
(   Environment:) Tj
T*
(   - Subhash Kumar v. State of Bihar \(1991\): The Supreme Court formally ruled that the Right to) Tj
T*
(   Life under Article 21 includes the fundamental right to the enjoyment of pollution-free water) Tj
T*
(   and air for full enjoyment of life.) Tj
T*
(   3. Integration of International Environmental Doctrines into Indian Law:) Tj
T*
(   - The Precautionary Principle and Polluter Pays Principle:) Tj
T*
(   In Vellore Citizens' Welfare Forum v. Union of India \(1996\), the Supreme Court held that the) Tj
T*
(   Precautionary Principle and the Polluter Pays Principle are essential features of Sustainable) Tj
T*
(   Development and are part of the environmental law of India under Article 21.) Tj
T*
(   - The Public Trust Doctrine:) Tj
T*
(   In M.C. Mehta v. Kamal Nath \(1997\), the Court imported the Roman law doctrine: Natural) Tj
T*
(   resources \(rivers, forests, seashores\) are held by the State in public trust for the collective) Tj
T*
(   benefit of the entire public, and the State cannot convert public trusts into private) Tj
T*
(   commercial ownership.) Tj
T*
(   - Absolute Liability Doctrine:) Tj
T*
(   In M.C. Mehta v. Union of India \(Oleum Gas Leak Case, 1987\), Justice P.N. Bhagwati formulated) Tj
T*
(   the principle of Absolute Liability: An enterprise engaged in a hazardous or inherently) Tj
T*
(   dangerous industry owes an absolute and non-delegable duty to the community, subject to zero) Tj
T*
(   exceptions \(unlike Rylands v. Fletcher strict liability\).) Tj
T*
