(   Impact Assessment Authority \(SEIAA\) upon recommendation of the State Expert Appraisal Committee) Tj
T*
(   \(SEAC\).) Tj
T*
(   3. The Four Sequential Stages of Environmental Clearance:) Tj
T*
(   - Stage 1: Screening \(Applicable to Category B Projects Only\):) Tj
T*
(   * The SEAC scrutinizes the preliminary Form 1 application to determine whether the proposed) Tj
T*
(   project requires full EIA studies \(categorized as Category B1\) or does not require an EIA study) Tj
T*
(   \(categorized as Category B2, which proceeds directly to appraisal\).) Tj
T*
(   * Category A projects bypass screening; a full comprehensive EIA is mandatory.) Tj
T*
(   - Stage 2: Scoping:) Tj
T*
(   * The Expert Appraisal Committee determines detailed, comprehensive Terms of Reference \(ToR\)) Tj
T*
(   addressing all potential environmental impacts \(air dispersion modeling, hydrogeological) Tj
T*
(   surveys, biodiversity assessment, socio-economic baseline, risk analysis\).) Tj
T*
(   * The project proponent conducts seasonal baseline field monitoring \(excluding monsoon\) to) Tj
T*
(   draft the comprehensive Environmental Impact Assessment \(EIA\) report and Environmental) Tj
T*
(   Management Plan \(EMP\).) Tj
T*
(   - Stage 3: Public Consultation:) Tj
T*
(   * Comprises two components: A formal public hearing conducted at the physical project site, and) Tj
T*
(   inviting written responses from stakeholders to ensure public participation and transparency.) Tj
T*
(   - Stage 4: Appraisal:) Tj
T*
(   * Detailed multidisciplinary scrutiny by the EAC/SEAC of the final EIA report, outcome of the) Tj
T*
(   public consultations, and the commitments made by the project proponent.) Tj
T*
(   * The EAC/SEAC makes formal recommendations to the regulatory authority \(MoEFCC/SEIAA\) to grant) Tj
T*
(   Environmental Clearance with specific environmental safeguards, or reject the application.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
