(   [Problem Statement]: Detail Earthquake Seismology, distinguishing P-waves, S-waves, and Surface waves, and formulate the Moment Magnitude Scale \(Mw\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Earthquake Seismology: Elastodynamics, Wave Propagation, and Moment Magnitude Scale:) Tj
T*
(   1. Elastodynamic Theory of Seismic Waves:) Tj
T*
(   An earthquake occurs when accumulated tectonic shear stress along a geological fault abruptly) Tj
T*
(   exceeds the frictional shear resistance of the rock interface \(Elastic Rebound Theory\). The) Tj
T*
(   sudden release of elastic strain energy radiates mechanical seismic waves through the) Tj
T*
(   lithosphere:) Tj
T*
(   2. Body Waves \(Propagating through Earth's Interior\):) Tj
T*
(   - Primary \(Compressional / P\) Waves:) Tj
T*
(   * Longitudinal elastic waves where rock particles oscillate parallel to the direction of wave) Tj
T*
(   propagation.) Tj
T*
(   * Propagate through both solids and fluids \(liquids and gases\).) Tj
T*
(   * Velocity: v_p = sqrt[ \(K + 4/3 * G\) / rho ]) Tj
T*
(   Where K is Bulk Modulus, G is Shear Modulus, and rho is rock density. Fastest seismic wave \(v_p) Tj
T*
(   ~ 5.5 to 8.0 km/s in crust; first to register on seismographs\).) Tj
T*
(   - Secondary \(Shear / S\) Waves:) Tj
T*
(   * Transverse elastic waves where rock particles oscillate perpendicular to the direction of) Tj
T*
(   wave propagation.) Tj
T*
(   * Because liquids and gases possess zero shear modulus \(G = 0\), S-waves CANNOT propagate) Tj
T*
(   through fluids! \(Proving the liquid nature of Earth's outer core\).) Tj
T*
(   * Velocity: v_s = sqrt[ G / rho ] ~ 3.0 to 4.5 km/s in crust.) Tj
T*
(   3. Surface Waves \(Propagating along Earth's Free Surface\):) Tj
T*
(   - Rayleigh Waves: Ground particles move in retrograde elliptical orbits in the vertical plane.) Tj
T*
(   Cause ground rolling; responsible for severe structural damage.) Tj
T*
(   - Love Waves: Transverse horizontal surface shaking. Highly destructive to building foundations) Tj
T*
(   lacking lateral ductility.) Tj
T*
(   4. The Moment Magnitude Scale \(M_w, Kanamori, 1977\):) Tj
T*
(   Unlike empirical Richter local magnitude \(M_L\) which saturates for large earthquakes \(M > 7.5\),) Tj
T*
(   the Moment Magnitude Scale is based directly on the physical Seismic Moment \(M_0\) of the) Tj
T*
(   rupture:) Tj
T*
(   M_0 = mu * A * D) Tj
T*
(   Where:) Tj
T*
(   - mu: Rock shear modulus \(typically 30 GPa = 3.0 * 10^10 N/m2 in crust\).) Tj
T*
(   - A: Total rupture area of the fault plane \(m2\).) Tj
T*
(   - D: Average fault dislocation slip displacement \(meters\).) Tj
T*
(   - Moment Magnitude Formula:) Tj
T*
(   M_w = 2/3 * log10\(M_0\) - 10.7 [where M_0 is measured in dyne-cm], or) Tj
T*
(   M_w = 2/3 * log10\(M_0\) - 6.07 [where M_0 is measured in N.m].) Tj
T*
(   Provides an unsaturated, continuous physical measurement of total seismic energy released.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
