(   [Problem Statement]: Formulate geotechnical slope stability, the Mohr-Coulomb failure criterion, Factor of Safety \(FoS\), and bioengineering slope stabilization.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Geotechnical Slope Stability, Mohr-Coulomb Mechanics, and Bioengineering Stabilization:) Tj
T*
(   1. Mechanics of Slope Equilibrium:) Tj
T*
(   A soil mass resting on a hillside inclined at angle beta experiences gravitational driving) Tj
T*
(   shear stress \(tau_d\) tending to pull the mass downslope, opposed by the internal shear strength) Tj
T*
(   \(tau_f\) of the soil resisting movement.) Tj
T*
(   2. The Mohr-Coulomb Shear Strength Criterion:) Tj
T*
(   tau_f = c' + sigma'_n * tan\(phi'\)) Tj
T*
(   Where:) Tj
T*
(   - c': Effective soil cohesion \(kPa\), representing electrostatic and chemical bonding between) Tj
T*
(   clay platelets.) Tj
T*
(   - sigma'_n: Effective normal stress perpendicular to the failure plane: sigma'_n = sigma_n - u) Tj
T*
(   \(where u is pore water pressure\).) Tj
T*
(   - phi': Effective internal angle of friction of the soil matrix \(degrees\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Factor of Safety \(FoS\) Formulations:) Tj
T*
(   FoS = \(Total Resisting Shear Strength\) / \(Total Mobilized Driving Shear Stress\) = tau_f / tau_d) Tj
T*
(   - For an infinite planar slope of depth z, soil unit weight gamma, and water table at surface) Tj
T*
(   \(saturated conditions\):) Tj
T*
(   FoS = [ c' + \(gamma_sat * z * cos^2\(beta\) - u\) * tan\(phi'\) ] / [ gamma_sat * z * sin\(beta\) *) Tj
T*
(   cos\(beta\) ]) Tj
T*
(   - Stability Classification:) Tj
T*
(   * FoS > 1.5: Geotechnically stable slope for long-term civil safety.) Tj
T*
(   * 1.0 < FoS < 1.3: Marginally stable; susceptible to failure during heavy monsoon storms.) Tj
T*
(   * FoS <= 1.0: Active shear failure and landslide initiation.) Tj
T*
(   4. Soil Bioengineering Slope Stabilization:) Tj
T*
(   - Vegetative Root Reinforcement: Planting deep-rooted perennial grasses \(Vetiveria zizanioides) Tj
T*
(   / Vetiver grass\) and pioneer trees. Vetiver roots possess extraordinary tensile strength \(~75) Tj
T*
(   MPa, equivalent to mild steel\) and penetrate 3 to 4 meters vertically, mechanically pinning) Tj
T*
(   unstable soil mantles to deeper bedrock.) Tj
T*
(   - Hydrological Amelioration: High vegetative transpiration draws down soil moisture, preventing) Tj
T*
(   positive pore water pressure buildup during heavy rainfall.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
